00\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates applicable:\n\u2022 5-yr survival for a GAAP-profitable, FCF-positive, refinanced-to-2030\n  software incumbent: ~90\u201395% [Assumption - base rate].\n\u2022 A 5%-growth SaaS reaching 20% GAAP operating margin within 4 years:\n  ~30\u201340% [Assumption - base rate for mature-software margin expansion].\n\u2022 UCaaS platform transition (core \u2192 AI/CCaaS attach) success: ~20\u201330%\n  [Assumption - platform-transition base rate]; RNG paid-AI ARR already\n  13% of ARR and doubling YoY [Actual - Q2'26 PR] \u2014 ahead of base rate but\n  small in absolute terms (~$0.1\u20130.2B ARR vs $2.6B revenue).\n\u2022 Mature-vendor NRR persistence >97%: ~70% [Assumption].\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation of my success probability (~50\u201355% for margin path)\n  from base rate (~30\u201340%): justified by (1) two consecutive years of GAAP\n  profitability with 3 consecutive quarterly guidance raises [Actual];\n  (2) SBC is STRUCTURALLY declining (10.7% of rev FY25 \u2192 9.2% guided FY26\n  \u2192 3\u20134% medium-term target) as high-priced 2021\u201322 grants vest out\n  [Actual - PR]; (3) refinancing completed to 2030 at lower rates with two\n  credit upgrades [Actual - PR]; (4) NRR ~99\u2013100% [Estimate - third-party\n  SEC-derived], validating retention. Deviation \u22641.5\u00d7 base rate \u2014 no flag.\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: my success probability (55%) < 1.5\u00d7\n  upper base rate (60%) \u2014 PASS, no re-evaluation required.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences): RingCentral sells a\n  cloud-native business phone/UC platform (voice, SMS, video, messaging)\n  and an expanding AI-enabled contact-center suite (RingCX, RingSense AI),\n  monetized as per-seat subscription (96% of revenue, ~$2.55B ARR run-rate).\n  It holds a leadership position in the PURE-PLAY UCaaS/CCaaS segment\n  (57.15% vs Five9 26.36% vs 8x8 16.49% [Estimate - csimarket peer set \u2014\n  this EXCLUDES Microsoft/Zoom/Cisco and therefore overstates true share]).\n  The business model is a subscription annuity with ~100% NRR: revenue\n  growth (~5%) now comes almost entirely from new-logos + AI attach, not\n  seat expansion. Understandability: **High**.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0\u20133 each) + direction:\n  Intangible assets (brand, 12-yr Gartner MQ Leader, TIME best-co) 3\n  Switching costs (phone migration, integrations, 911/telecom complexity) 3\n  Network effects (global voice network, carrier interconnects)         2\n  Cost advantage (infrastructure scale)                                 2\n  Economies of scale (SBC/margin leverage)                               2\n  Regulatory barriers (telecom compliance, data sovereignty)            2\n  Data moat (conversation data \u2192 AI, RingSense)                          2\n  TOTAL 16/21 \u2192 **Fortress (low end)** [Estimate - subjective scoring].\n  Moat DIRECTION: **Stable** \u2014 UCaaS core Stable-to-Narrowing (Microsoft\n  Teams Phone bundling at near-zero marginal price; Zoom price pressure\n  [Estimate - research brief]), offset by Widening AI/contact-center\n  franchise (13% of ARR on paid AI, doubling YoY [Actual]).\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: **Stalwart** \u2014 ~5% growth, dominant share,\n  dividend initiator. Checkpoints: margin stability (improving, guided),\n  pricing power vs Microsoft (the core risk), buyback discipline (good\n  recently: 2.2M sh @ ~$43 in Q2'26 [Actual - PR]).\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:\n  \u2022 Rev growth: 3-yr CAGR 8.2% [Arithmetic], last-4Q +5.9% [Actual].\n  \u2022 Op margin: 3-yr avg \u2248 -1.4% [Arithmetic], last-4Q \u2248 7.1% [Estimate],\n    FY26 guided 9.0\u20139.7% GAAP [Actual - PR].\n  \u2022 EPS: FY25 GAAP $0.47 [Arithmetic]; TTM $1.32 [Estimate]; FY26E ~$1.74\n    [Estimate]; non-GAAP EPS FY26E $4.96\u20135.10 [Actual - guidance].\n  \u2022 ROE: N/A (negative equity). ROIC (GAAP basis) \u2248 18\u201320% [Estimate \u2014\n    distorted by negative equity base; see 2.5].\n  \u2022 FCF margin 23.3% FY25 [Arithmetic]; FCF/OCF conversion ~95% [Arithmetic].\n  \u2022 Net debt/EBITDA \u2248 1.5\u20131.6x [Estimate]; GAAP interest coverage \u2248 4.1x\n    FY26E [Estimate]; Adj-EBITDA coverage \u2248 11x [Estimate].\n  \u2022 Net debt $1.09B vs equity -$0.588B \u2192 levered on book basis, but the\n    equity base is an artifact of a $1.7B accumulated deficit [Actual].\n  GRADE: **Good (improving)**. Piotroski 8/9; accruals -0.35 (cash-heavy\n  earnings) [Arithmetic - pack].\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each / 15):\n  Buyback efficiency: 2 \u2014 recent buys at ~$43 avg (below T0 $66.71,\n  $326M remaining [Actual]) BUT 2021\u201322 dilution was heavy (SBC 19\u201322% of\n  revenue).\n  M&A track record: 2 \u2014 no major recent deals; 2021\u201324 Mitel-era\n  partnership wound down; conservative.\n  Capex efficiency: 3 \u2014 capex only ~3.3% of revenue for a cash machine.\n  Mix rationality: 2 \u2014 dividend ($0.125/q, +67% [Actual]) + buyback + debt\n  paydown ($85M in Q2'26 [Actual]) + SBC cut = coherent, aligned.\n  Guidance accuracy: 3 \u2014 beat-and-raise 3 consecutive quarters [Actual].\n  Insider bonus: 0 (insider ownership only 1.27% [Aggregator]; CEO-founder\n  present but <5% disclosed; 6-mo insider net buys [Unconfirmed]).\n  TOTAL 12/15 \u2014 strong.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1/5. R&D capitalization [Estimate - all of this block]:\n  R&D assumed ~24% of revenue (SaaS norm; RNG does not break it out in the\n  pack). FY25 R&D \u2248 $0.60B; 3-yr straight-line amortization of FY23\u201325\n  R&D \u2248 $0.17B/yr. Adjusted op income \u2248 $0.121B + $0.60B \u2212 $0.17B \u2248\n  $0.55B (FY25). Adjusted invested capital \u2248 reported IC (~$0.47B, net\n  debt + negative equity) + unamortized R&D (~$1.4B) \u2248 $1.9B.\n  Adjusted ROIC \u2248 22% vs GAAP-ROIC \u2248 18\u201320% \u2192 the >5pp gap rule does NOT\n  trigger; both are strong. The real story: RNG's economics improve once\n  you stop expensing growth investment \u2014 supports the quality narrative.\nStep 2.5-2. SBC treatment (the material adjustment): Adjusted FCF =\n  Reported FCF \u2212 SBC. FY25: $0.587B \u2212 $0.270B = **$0.317B** [Arithmetic].\n  FY26E: guided FCF $0.620B \u2212 guided SBC $0.243B \u2248 **$0.377B** [Arithmetic\n  on company guidance]. SBC is expensed in my DCF via GAAP NOPAT (no\n  double-count, no add-back) \u2014 per framework Layer 2.5.\nStep 2.5-3. Leases: operating leases $0.036B (FY25 [Actual]) capitalized\n  into net debt (included in my $1.09B figure).\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett), FY25 [Estimate]: NI $0.043B + D&A\n  $0.223B \u2212 maintenance capex $0.087B (incl. software) \u2212 \u0394WC \u2248 $0.18B.\n  Note: management's \"$7/share FCF\" is OCF-minus-capex and adds back SBC;\n  an owner who treats SBC as compensation must use ~$0.26\u20130.32B instead.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1 (DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION (each input sourced):\n  rf = 4.70% [Actual - FRED DGS10 4.74 / CNBC 4.70, Aug-2026]\n  ERP = 5.0% [Assumption - standard long-horizon U.S. equity premium]\n  \u03b2 = 1.15 [Assumption - adjusted up from vendor 1.117 for financial\n      leverage ($1.09B net debt) and realized drawdown volatility]\n  Cost of equity = 4.70 + 1.15 \u00d7 5.0 = **10.45%**\n  Pre-tax Kd = 5.5% [Estimate - restated credit facility (SOFR+~250bp,\n      $310M term loan rate-reduced; $609M 2026 converts at 1.50% retired\n      [Actual - company PRs])]; after-tax (22%) = 4.3%\n  Weights: E = $5.57B (83.6%), D = $1.09B (16.4%) [Arithmetic]\n  **WACC = 0.836 \u00d7 10.45% + 0.164 \u00d7 4.3% \u2248 9.4% \u2192 used 9.5%** (round-up\n  for conservatism; 50% CI 9.0\u201310.0%, 80% CI 8.5\u201310.5% [Assumption]).\n\nFORECAST PATH (FY2026E\u2013FY2030E) \u2014 every driver sourced:\n| FY | Rev $B | Rev gr | GAAP op mgn | OpInc $M | D&A $M | Capex $M | NOPAT $M | FCFF $M |\n| 2026E| 2.640 | +5.0% | 9.4% | 248 | 220 | 87 | 193 | 301 |\n| 2027E| 2.759 | +4.5% | 12.5% | 345 | 210 | 91 | 269 | 363 |\n| 2028E| 2.869 | +4.0% | 16.0% | 459 | 200 | 95 | 358 | 438 |\n| 2029E| 2.970 | +3.5% | 18.5% | 549 | 180 | 98 | 428 | 490 |\n| 2030E| 3.058 | +3.0% | 20.0% | 612 | 160 | 101 | 477 | 521 |\n\u2022 Revenue: FY26 = guidance midpoint $2.6405B [Actual - Q2'26 PR];\n  FY27\u201330 fade 4.5%\u21923.0% [Assumption, grounded in: management admission\n  of \"mid-single digits\" [Estimate - TipRanks]; sell-side models ~4.5%\n  through 2027 [Estimate - TIKR]; KoalaGains model +5% CAGR FY25\u201329\n  [Estimate]; consensus forward rev growth 4.5% [Aggregator]].\n\u2022 GAAP op margin: FY26 = guidance 9.0\u20139.7% [Actual]; path to 20.0% by\n  2030 [Assumption \u2014 slightly behind management's \"2\u20133 years\" pace\n  [Actual - call excerpt], deliberately conservative]. Driver: SBC\n  collapse 10.7%\u2192~3.5% of revenue (guided 3\u20134% medium-term [Actual]) plus\n  G&A leverage. Non-GAAP margin guide 23.6\u201324% FY26 [Actual] implies\n  GAAP/non-GAAP gap \u2248 SBC + acquired-intangible amortization \u2248 14pp \u2014 my\n  path is mechanically consistent.\n\u2022 D&A: $220M FY26 declining to $160M by FY30 [Assumption \u2014 flat 2023\u201325\n  at $223\u2013234M [Actual - pack]; acquired-intangible amortization rolls\n  off ~2028\u201330; NOTE: if D&A does NOT roll off, terminal FCFF falls ~$70M\n  \u2248 -$6/share).\n\u2022 Capex: 3.3% of revenue (~$87M FY26) [Assumption \u2014 pack flags filed\n  $30M (PP&E only) vs vendor $87M (incl. capitalized software); I chose\n  the broader, more conservative basis and flag it; the company's own\n  quarterly FCF bridge (OCF $206M \u2212 FCF $180M = $26M in Q2'26) is\n  inconsistent with FY25's $30M \u2014 unresolved [Unconfirmed]].\n\u2022 Tax: 22.0% [Assumption \u2014 matches company non-GAAP tax rate ~22.5%\n  [Actual - PR]; GAAP rate may be lower via NOLs but pack tax detail is\n  withheld, so I carry 22% conservatively].\n\u2022 \u0394WC: -$25M\u2192-$15M/yr (working-capital source of cash from negative WC\n  as revenue grows) [Assumption; FY25's -$576M swing is a DEBT\n  RECLASSIFICATION artifact (2026 converts moved to current), not\n  operating \u2014 excluded].\n\u2022 Terminal: g = 2.5% (< nominal GDP, below WACC \u2713) [Assumption];\n  terminal margin 20% (management target); terminal capex \u2248 D&A \u2248 $110M\n  (normalization, avoids the \"D&A > capex forever\" error).\n\nDCF RESULT (WACC 9.5%):\n  PV FCFF 2026\u201330 = 275+303+334+341+331 = **$1,584M** [Arithmetic]\n  TV = ($521M\u00d71.025)/(0.095\u22120.025) = $7,629M; PV = **$4,847M**\n  EV = **$6,431M**; less net debt $1,093M (LT $630M + current $624M \u2212\n  $85M Q2'26 paydown \u2212 cash $112M + op-leases $36M [Actual/Arithmetic])\n  \u21d2 Equity = **$5,338M**; \u00f7 85.0M shares (83.5M out + dilution net of\n  buybacks [Assumption, consistent with company's 86.5\u201387.0M FY26 diluted\n  basis]) \u21d2 **IV \u2248 $63/share**.\n  Sensitivity: WACC 9.0% \u2192 ~$70; WACC 10.0% \u2192 ~$58; margin 20% by 2029\n  (management pace) \u2192 ~$72; margin stalls at 15% \u2192 ~$50. Top-3 tornado\n  drivers: (1) 2030 GAAP op margin, (2) WACC, (3) revenue growth path \u2014\n  these become the thesis KPIs.\n\nMULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK (not the conclusion, per Rule 2):\n  EV/Revenue FY26E 2.5x; EV/Adj-EBITDA FY26E ~9.4x; P/Company-FCF 9.0x;\n  P/Owner-FCF (after SBC) ~14.8x; forward non-GAAP P/E 13.3x ($66.71 /\n  $5.03 guide midpoint); GAAP P/E 2028E ~18x, 2029E ~14.6x, 2030E ~12.8x\n  [Arithmetic on my forecast]. Trailing GAAP P/E 54x and P/B -9.3 are\n  meaningless at the current inflection. Multiple check: RNG is NOT cheap\n  on owner-FCF for 5% growth; it is cheap only if margins re-rate as\n  guided. Neutral-to-slightly-rich on multiples.\n\nREVERSE DCF (what T0 embeds): at $66.71 (EV $6.66B), WACC 9.5%, g 2.5%,\n  my cost structure: the market prices ~18.5\u201319% GAAP op margin by 2030 \u2014\n  i.e., ~95\u2013100% of my base case with ~zero margin of safety. No\n  expectations gap in either direction [Arithmetic].\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\u2022 Competitive response (-1): if RNG's AI attach succeeds, Microsoft\n  (Teams), Zoom, Five9, NICE will respond with bundled AI/voice pricing \u2014\n  the pure-play 57% \"share\" is inside a pond Microsoft can flood.\n\u2022 Reflexivity (positive loop active): high stock \u2192 cheaper SBC (fewer\n  shares needed to compensate) \u2192 SBC target achievable \u2192 higher GAAP\n  margins \u2192 re-rating. The negative loop (falling stock \u2192 SBC re-inflates\n  \u2192 margin target missed) is the bear's mechanism.\n\u2022 Customer evolution: mainstream/late-adopter market; NRR ~99\u2013100% shows\n  lock-in but low expansion; AI attach (13% of ARR, doubling) is the only\n  up-sell vector of size; watch seat-cannibalization (AI agents replacing\n  paid human seats) \u2014 a genuine long-term demand risk.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (Bayesian)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nProbabilities sum to 100%; base case is the single highest probability.\n\u2022 BEAR (25%): growth fades to ~2.5\u20133% (NRR <95%, AI cannibalization,\n  Microsoft price war), SBC stays ~8% of rev (comp pressure), GAAP margin\n  stalls ~13\u201314%; WACC 10.2%. Price \u2248 **$46\u201348** (12\u201313x 2029 GAAP EPS).\n\u2022 BASE (55%): my forecast \u2014 margin 20% by 2030, growth fades 5%\u21923%.\n  IV \u2248 **$63\u201365**.\n\u2022 BULL (20%): AI re-accelerates revenue to ~7% through 2028, GAAP margin\n  20% by 2029 (management pace), SBC \u2192 3.5%, WACC 9.0%. Price \u2248\n  **$85\u201390** (17x 2029 GAAP EPS ~$5.0).\n\u2022 Bayesian update: prior success prob (from 1